@* FR-117's two states, named once.

   A shift is running or it is finished, and both screens say so - two copies of one vocabulary
   otherwise, and the kind of duplication where the second copy is the one that goes stale.

   The words are the shift's and deliberately not the driver's. The two driver pickers say "на
   зміні" and "не на зміні", which are claims about where somebody is now; this badge labels a row
   in a history, where last Tuesday's shift is finished and says nothing about whether its driver is
   working today. Sharing one pair of keys would have put a present-tense claim about a person in a
   column headed "стан зміни", and it would have been false for every row but the open one.

   Independent @@if blocks rather than a ternary or an if/else chain: an `else` at markup level reads
   to NFR-14's source scan as a rendered English word, and a key composed at run time would be a key
   the closed-catalogue test cannot see. The two literal localizer lookups below are what make those
   two keys reachable.

   The colours are the themed Bootstrap badges' rather than values written here (AD-28, NFR-29):
   `text-bg-success` and `text-bg-secondary` already wear the palette, so there is no hex anywhere in
   this file and nothing for the design-token gate to catch. *@
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

@if (IsOpen)
{
    <span class="badge text-bg-success">@Localizer["ShiftRunning"]</span>
}
@if (!IsOpen)
{
    <span class="badge text-bg-secondary">@Localizer["ShiftFinished"]</span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the shift is still running. A bool rather than a status enum, because there is no
    /// status column to mirror: open is <c>EndedAt IS NULL</c>, which is also the filter of the
    /// index enforcing one open shift per driver (FR-110, FR-117).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public bool IsOpen { get; set; }
}
